<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enylus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 333–332 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enylus was the last known independent king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> under Persian rule in the fourth century BCE, known from his coins and from the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>. In 332 BCE, learning that Byblos had already submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, he left the Persian fleet and brought his ships to join Alexander at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>.</p></div>
